GPIO stands for General-Purpose Input/Outpu. It allows the MCU to communicate with external
hardware such as LEDs, buttons, sensors, relays, and other digital devices.

- In STM32F407 GPIO ports are named GPIOA through GPIOI on the standard STM32F407 device family.
- Each available port has 16 pins: Pin 0 through Pin 15
- Each GPIO port has seven primary registers used for configuration and data access.

1. Primary GPIO Registers

 Register            Purpose               			 		 Key concept
  MODER			  Selects pin mode       				Input, output, alternate function, analog
  OTYPER		  Selects output type					Push-pull or open-drain
  OSPEEDR		  Controls output speed 				Low, medium, high, very high
  PUPDR			  Configures internal pull resistors    No pull, pull-up, pull-down
  IDR             Reads input levels                    Read-only
  ODR			  Stores output levels					Read/write
  BSRR			  Sets or resets output bits			Atomic bit set/reset
  
  
2. Understand Each Register
A. MODER - GPIO Mode Register
   - Each pin uses two bits.
   
   Bits 		Mode
   00			Input
   01 			General-Purpose Outpu
   10			Alternate Function
   11			Analog
   
   Example: Configure PA5 as an output
   
   GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 10);  // Clear PA5 mode bits
   GPIOA->MODER |=  (1U << 10);  // Set output mode (01)
   
   Sample:
   For STM32 GPIOX->MODER, each pin gets 2 bits.
	All 32 bits in MODER Register get assigned to PX0 to PX15.
	
	For instance, GPIOA Port:
	
	PINS  MODER Bits      Shifts       Clear Expression
	PA0	-> [1:0]     		0			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 0); 3 in Binary: 11
	PA1	-> [3:2]			2			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 2);
	PA2	-> [5:4]			4			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 4);
	PA3	-> [7:6]			6			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 6);
	PA4	-> [9:8]			8			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 8);
	PA5	-> [11:10]			10			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 10);
	PA6	-> [13:12]			12			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 12);
	PA7	-> [15:14]			14			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 14);
	PA8	-> [17:16]			16			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 16);
	PA9	-> [19:18]			18			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 18);
	PA10-> [21:20]			20			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 20);
	PA11-> [23:22]			22			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 22);
	PA12-> [25:24]			24			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 24);
	PA13-> [27:26]			26			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 26);
	PA14-> [29:28]			28			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 28);
	PA15-> [31:30]			30 			GPIOA->MODER &= ~(3U << 30);
	
	For expression such as clear: we can do:
	
	PIN_NO = 1;
	GPIO->MODER &= ~(3U << (PIN_NO * 2));
	   
B. OTYPER - Output type register
   - Each pin uses one bit.
   
   Bit			Function
   0			Push-pull: actively drives HIGH and LOW
   1			Open-drain: actively drives LOW but requires a pull-up to achieve HIGH
   
   Example:
   
   GPIOA->OTYPER &= ~(1U << 5);  // PA5 push-pull
   GPIOA->OTYPER |=  (1U << 5);  // PA5 open-drain
   
C. OSPEEDR - GPIO Output Speed Register
   - Each pins uses 2 bits.
   - Controls output transition speed (slew rate), not the CPU clock frequency. 
   
   Bits 		Mode
   00			Low
   01			Medium
   10 			High
   11			Very High
   
   Example: Configure PA5 for High Speed
   
   GPIOA->OSPEEDR &= ~(3U << 10); // PA5 (5 * 2) - Clear PA5 Speed Bits. 3U (dec) to 11 (binary)
   GPIOA->OSPEEDR |= (2U << 10).  // PA5 (5 * 2) - Set High Speed ( 2U (dec) to 10 (binary))
   
D. PUPDR - GPIO Pull-Up/Pull-Down Register
   - Each pin uses 2 bits.
   - Defines the internal pull resistor configuration.
   
   Bits 		Configuration 
   00 			No pull-up or pull-down
   01			Pull-up
   10 			Pull-down
   11			Reserved
   
   Example: Enable pull-up on PA5
   
   GPIOA->PUPDR &= ~(3U << 10); // Clear PA5 pull bits
   GPIOA->PUPDR |= (1U << 10);  // Enable pull-up
   
E. IDR - Input Data Register 
   - Each pin uses 1 bit.
   - Reads the current logic level at the GPIO input.
   
   Bit 		Value
   0		LOW
   1 		HIGH
   
   Example: Read PA5
   
   if (GPIOA->IDR & (1U << 5))
   {
   		// PA5 is HIGH
   }
   else
   {
   		// PA5 is LOW
   }

F. ODR - Output Data Register
   - Each pin uses 1 bit.
   - Stores the output level driven by the GPIO when configured as an output.
   
   Bit 		Function
   0 		Drive LOW
   1 		Drive HIGH
   
   Example: Set or clear PA5
   
   GPIOA->ODR |= (1U << 5); // Set PA5 HIGH
   GPIOA->ODR &= ~(1U << 5); // Set PA5 LOW
   
G. BSRR - Bit Set/Reset Register
   - A 32-bit write-only register on many STM32 families.
   - The lower 16 bits set pins; the upper 16 bits reset pins. 
   - Allow atomic pin updates without a read-modify-write operation on ODR.
   
   Bits			Function
   0 - 15		Set Corresponding pin HIGH
   16 - 32		Reset Corresponding pin LOW
   
   Example: Set and reset PA5
   
   GPIOA->BSRR = (1U << 5); // Set PA5 HIGH
   GPIOA->BSRR = (1U << (5 + 16)); // Reset PA5 LOW
   
H. LCKR - GPIO Configuration Lock Register
   - Locks selected GPIO pin configuration until a reset, using a lock sequence.
   - Helps prevent accidental changes to protected pin configurations
   
   Bits			Function
   0 - 15		Select pins to lock
   16			Lock key bit
   
   Example: Lock PA5 Configuration
   
   GPIOA->LCKR = (1U << 5) | (1U << 16);
   GPIOA->LCKR = (1U << 5);
   GPIOA->LCKR = (1U << 5) | (1U << 16);
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   
   